    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h2 id="corporate-tax-rates-overview" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. Headline Corporate Tax Rate (24%) vs SME Rates</h2>
    <p>
      In Malaysia, companies incorporated under the Companies Act 2016 (such as a Sdn Bhd or Berhad) are subject to corporate income tax on statutory income derived from or accrued in Malaysia. Income remitted into Malaysia from foreign sources has historically seen changing treatments, but domestic business profits remain the primary target of taxation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The headline corporate tax rate is a flat <strong>24%</strong>. This applies uniformly to large corporations and non-resident companies. However, to spur domestic economic growth and support the backbone of the economy, the Malaysian government has cemented a highly attractive preferential tax regime for Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs). Qualifying SMEs enjoy a reduced rate of <strong>17%</strong> on the first RM 600,000 of their chargeable income. Any income exceeding this RM 600,000 threshold is subsequently taxed at the standard 24% rate.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Corporate Tax Tiers -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
