          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>The Financial Impact:</strong> For an SME maximizing the RM 600,000 threshold, the 17% rate generates a direct tax savings of RM 42,000 compared to a company paying a flat 24% on the same amount. This retained capital is vital for hiring, software acquisition, or expanding operations.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h2 id="sme-tax-incentive-qualifications" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. SME 17% Tax Tier Qualification Criteria</h2>
    <p>
      The term "SME" is loosely used in banking and business, but the Inland Revenue Board has a very strict, mathematically precise definition for tax purposes. To legitimately enjoy the preferential 17% corporate tax rate on the first RM 600,000 of statutory income, a company must satisfy three mandatory, non-negotiable conditions simultaneously:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-4 text-sm my-4">
      <li>
        <strong>Paid-up Capital Requirement:</strong> The company's paid-up capital in respect of ordinary shares must not exceed RM 2.5 million at the beginning of the basis period for a Year of Assessment (YA). If you inject capital that pushes the total to RM 2,500,001 on the first day of your financial year, you lose the SME status entirely for that year.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gross Sales Turnover Limit:</strong> The total annual gross business turnover must not exceed RM 50 million for the basis period. This condition ensures that the SME rate benefits genuinely smaller operations rather than massive trading houses with low paid-up capital.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No Related Corporate Shareholding (Anti-Avoidance Rule):</strong> The company cannot be part of a larger corporate group where a related company has a paid-up capital exceeding RM 2.5 million. Specifically, if more than 50% of the SME's shares are held by another company with >RM 2.5m capital, or if both companies are controlled by the same parent holding company, the SME tax privilege is forfeited. This prevents large corporations from fragmenting into multiple RM 2.5m capital shell companies to exploit the 17% rate.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Failing any single one of these criteria immediately subjects the company to the flat 24% tax rate from the very first Ringgit earned.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <h2 id="entity-comparison-sdn-bhd-vs-sole-prop" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">3. Entity Tax Comparison: Sdn Bhd vs Sole Proprietorship</h2>
    <p>
      When founding a business in Malaysia, entrepreneurs frequently grapple with choosing between a <strong>Sdn Bhd (Private Limited Company)</strong> and a <strong>Sole Proprietorship / Partnership</strong> (Enterprise). The choice has profound tax and liability implications. Empathy for founders here is crucial—it's stressful deciding how your hard-earned profits will be taxed.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <span class="rounded bg-primary/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-primary">Sdn Bhd (Company)</span>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mt-2 mb-1 dark:text-on-primary">Flat Corporate Tax (17% / 24%)</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted mb-2">
          A Sdn Bhd is a separate legal entity. The profit generated is taxed at the corporate level (17% for SMEs up to RM600k, then 24%).
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted font-semibold">
          Advantage: Malaysia uses a single-tier tax system. Once the company pays its corporate tax, any remaining profit distributed to directors/shareholders as dividends is 100% tax-free in the hands of the individual.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <span class="rounded bg-emerald-500/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-emerald-600">Sole Proprietor / Partnership</span>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mt-2 mb-1 dark:text-on-primary">Personal Progressive Tax (0% - 30%)</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted mb-2">
          Enterprises are not taxed as separate entities. All net business profits "pass-through" directly to the owner and are declared in their personal Form B.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted font-semibold">
          Disadvantage: High profitability pushes the individual into the highest personal tax brackets (up to 30%). A highly profitable Sole Prop often pays significantly more tax overall than an SME Sdn Bhd.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      For a business netting RM 500,000 a year, a sole proprietor might hit the 28-30% bracket for a large chunk of their income, whereas a Sdn Bhd would lock in that income at a comfortable 17%, allowing for much more aggressive reinvestment strategies.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <h2 id="withholding-tax-section-107a-109b" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">4. Section 107A & Section 109B Withholding Tax</h2>
    <p>
      As businesses digitize and expand, paying foreign contractors, software providers, or consultants has become standard practice. Malaysia enforces strict Withholding Tax (WHT) rules to ensure tax is collected before funds leave the country. When a Malaysian company pays a non-resident, it must withhold a specific percentage and remit it to LHDNM within 30 days. Failure to do so not only attracts late payment penalties but also renders the entire expense non-deductible for the Malaysian company.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-4 text-sm my-4">
      <li>
        <strong>Section 107A Withholding Tax (Contract Payments):</strong> This applies to non-resident contractors performing services inside Malaysia. It requires a 13% withholding deduction—comprising 10% held on account of the non-resident company's potential tax liability, and 3% held on account of the non-resident's employees' potential tax.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Section 109B Withholding Tax (Special Classes of Income):</strong> This is incredibly common for modern digital businesses. It imposes a 10% withholding tax on payments for technical advice, assistance, installation services, or management fees paid to non-residents. Furthermore, royalty payments (e.g., software licensing) fall under Section 109, also generally subject to 10% WHT.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Expert Tip:</em> Always check if a Double Taxation Agreement (DTA) exists between Malaysia and the payee's home country. DTAs can often reduce withholding tax rates from 10% down to 8%, 5%, or even 0% in specific circumstances, provided you obtain the necessary Certificate of Residence (COR) from the payee.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <h2 id="cp58-commission-statement" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">5. Form CP58 Agent & Distributor Commission Declarations</h2>
    <p>
      Another heavy compliance burden for businesses operating MLM (Multi-Level Marketing) models, real estate agencies, or extensive dealer networks is the <strong>Form CP58</strong>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Under LHDNM regulations, companies paying monetary commissions or non-monetary incentives (such as all-expenses-paid incentive trips to Europe, luxury watches, or cars) to local agents, dealers, or distributors must declare these payouts. If the total value of monetary and non-monetary incentives exceeds <strong>RM 5,000 per year</strong> per agent, the company is legally mandated to issue a Form CP58 statement to the agent by March 31 of the following year.
    </p>
    <p>
      This allows LHDNM to cross-reference the company’s claimed expenses against the individual agent’s declared income, closing a massive historical tax loophole. Failure to prepare and issue CP58 on time can result in fines and the disallowance of those commission expenses during the company's tax audit.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <h2 id="capital-allowances-deductions" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">6. Capital Allowances vs Non-Deductible Expenses</h2>
    <p>
      A common area of confusion for new founders involves asset depreciation. <strong>Accounting depreciation is entirely non-deductible for tax purposes in Malaysia.</strong> You must add back the depreciation expense when computing your adjusted income. Instead, companies claim statutory <strong>Capital Allowances (CA)</strong> set by the government.
    </p>
    <p>
      Capital Allowances are divided into an Initial Allowance (IA) claimed in the year of purchase, and an Annual Allowance (AA) claimed every year until the asset is fully written off.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-3 my-6 font-mono text-sm">
      <div class="rounded-lg bg-canvas p-4 border border-hairline dark:bg-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800">
        <div class="font-bold text-primary font-sans mb-1">ICT Equipment & Computers</div>
        <code>Initial Allowance: 20% | Annual Allowance: 40%</code>
        <p class="font-sans text-xs mt-2 text-muted">Because of the high AA, computers are effectively fully written off for tax purposes over just 2 years (20% + 40% + 40%).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg bg-canvas p-4 border border-hairline dark:bg-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800">
        <div class="font-bold text-primary font-sans mb-1">Office Machinery & Commercial Plant</div>
        <code>Initial Allowance: 20% | Annual Allowance: 14%</code>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg bg-canvas p-4 border border-hairline dark:bg-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800">
        <div class="font-bold text-primary font-sans mb-1">Motor Vehicles (Company Cars)</div>
        <code>Initial Allowance: 20% | Annual Allowance: 20%</code>
        <p class="font-sans text-xs mt-2 text-muted">Strict limits apply to non-commercial passenger vehicles (often capped at RM 50k or RM 100k depending on the car's price when new).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

